    "created_at": "2020-01-04T11:54:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-01-04T11:54:38Z",
    "body": "shouldn't it be named `requestDevices` ?\r\n\r\nThere is something inconsistent in the IDL vs all resources/implementations, unless I missed something:\r\n\r\n- in the current spec as shown in https://wicg.github.io/webhid/ the example seems to indicate that it's a `HIDDevice` and not a `HIDDevice[]`:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nlet requestButton = document.getElementById('request-hid-device');\r\nrequestButton.addEventListener('click', async () => {\r\n  let device;\r\n  try {\r\n    device = await navigator.hid.requestDevice({ filters: [{\r\n        vendorId: 0xABCD,\r\n        productId: 0x1234,\r\n        usagePage: 0x0C,\r\n        usage: 0x01\r\n    }]});\r\n  } catch (error) {\r\n    console.log('No device was selected.');\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  if (device !== undefined) {\r\n    console.log('HID: ${device.productName}');\r\n  }\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n- current Chrome/Chromium implementation have `requestDevice` returning a single device, not a sequence.\r\n\r\n- WebUSB also have a `requestDevice` and it returns a single Device ( https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/USB/requestDevice )\r\n\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
